    <div class="bar"><a href="{{ route('character-pass') }}">← Character Pass</a><span>Complete sample · self-initiated</span></div>
    <section class="intro"><div><div class="eyebrow">Sample subject · BenchCue for Shopify</div><h1>Find the work inside the software.</h1></div><p>BenchCue already had a coherent page and visual system. The pass does not propose decoration for decoration’s sake. It identifies the one idea that should become harder to miss: this software exists at the boundary between a digital order and a physical bench.</p></section>
    <section class="score"><div><strong>Strong</strong><span>Existing visual voice</span></div><div><strong>Blurred</strong><span>First-screen category clarity</span></div><div><strong>Missing</strong><span>Physical consequence</span></div></section>
    <section class="diagnosis"><div><div class="eyebrow">01 · Visual diagnosis</div><h2>What the page is almost saying.</h2></div><div class="findings">
        <article class="finding"><div><h3>The useful boundary is buried.</h3><p>“Custom order details” is accurate, but generic. The distinctive moment is the handoff from Shopify’s order screen to someone engraving, printing, sewing, or assembling a real object.</p></div></article>
        <article class="finding"><div><h3>The screenshot proves interface, not consequence.</h3><p>The current hero shows that the extension exists. It does not yet let a maker feel the relief of carrying one clean instruction sheet to the bench.</p></div></article>
        <article class="finding"><div><h3>The first action delays the decision.</h3><p>“See the workflow” is appropriate while App Store review is pending. At launch, the hero should name Shopify immediately and let qualified merchants recognize themselves before asking them to study the process.</p></div></article>
    </div></section>
    <section class="direction"><div class="directionHead"><div><div class="eyebrow">02 · Focused concept</div><h2>Digital order. Physical certainty.</h2></div><p>The visual direction keeps BenchCue’s warm workshop palette, but makes the production handoff the central image. This is a direction study for the early page—not a claim that unmeasured conversion improved.</p></div>
        <div class="concept"><div class="conceptNav"><span>BENCHCUE / SHOPIFY</span><span>$7 monthly · 7 days free</span></div><div class="conceptHero"><div class="conceptCopy"><div><div class="eyebrow">For made-to-order Shopify shops</div><h3>Stop carrying the whole order to the workbench.</h3><p>BenchCue pulls the item, quantity, SKU, and public customization choices into one maker-ready production sheet.</p></div><div><span class="action">Install from Shopify →</span></div></div><div class="bench"><img src="https://maker-card.revertcreations.com/benchcue-production-sheet.png" alt="BenchCue production sheet showing a fictional customized product order"><p>ORDER → CUE → MAKE</p></div></div></div>
    </section>
    <section class="edit"><div><div class="eyebrow">03 · Prioritized edit plan</div><h2>Preserve the system. Change the emphasis.</h2></div><ol class="edits"><li><b>01</b><span>Name Shopify and the made-to-order audience in the first six words.</span></li><li><b>02</b><span>Replace the interface-only hero crop with the full production sheet as a physical-looking artifact.</span></li><li><b>03</b><span>Move the privacy boundary directly beneath the first product proof; it answers the most consequential trust question.</span></li><li><b>04</b><span>After publication, replace the workflow CTA with the actual App Store action. Until then, retain the honest review-state language.</span></li></ol></section>
    <section class="limit"><h2>What this sample does not pretend.</h2><p>This is a qualitative visual-direction study of a Revert Creations product. It uses no private analytics, customer research, or conversion experiment. It does not prove that the proposed direction will sell more. Its job is narrower: make a defensible idea visible enough to build and test.</p></section>
